\details{
\strong{Method}

Let \eqn{X} be the normalized table, \eqn{m_k} the mean of group \eqn{k}
(of \eqn{n_k} samples, with prior \eqn{\pi_k}), and
\eqn{\bar{x} = \sum_k \pi_k m_k}{xbar = sum(pi_k m_k)}. With \eqn{W}
the pooled within-group covariance (each sample minus its group's mean,
divisor \eqn{n - g}) and \eqn{B} the between-group covariance
\eqn{\sum_k n \pi_k (m_k - \bar{x})(m_k - \bar{x})^T / (g - 1)}{sum(n pi_k (m_k - xbar)(m_k - xbar)') / (g - 1)}
(with the default priors, \eqn{n \pi_k = n_k}), the discriminant axes
\eqn{a} maximize \eqn{a^T B a / a^T W a}, the ratio of the
between-group to the within-group variance of the scores (Fisher 1936).
They are the eigenvectors of \eqn{W^{-1} B}, and the ratios its
eigenvalues, \code{eig}. Each axis is scaled so that the pooled within-group
variance of its scores is 1, and the scores are
\eqn{(x - \bar{x})^T a}{(x - xbar)' a}, for every sample \eqn{x}.

As in scikit-learn's \code{"svd"} solver, \eqn{W} and \eqn{B} are never
formed: the axes come from the SVD of the within-group residuals (each
feature scaled by its within-group standard deviation), which spheres
\eqn{W}, then the SVD of the weighted group means in that sphered
space. Axes whose singular value is below \eqn{10^{-4}} times the first
are dropped, as both references do.

\strong{Singular tables}

LDA needs \eqn{W} to be invertible: more samples than features plus
groups, and no feature that is constant within every group or a linear
combination of others within groups (as relative abundances that add up
to 1 are). Otherwise \code{lda()} stops with an error (scikit-learn and
\code{MASS::lda()} drop the null directions with a warning, but the axes
they then report depend on rounding). Microbiome tables have far more
taxa than samples, so use \code{\link[=capda]{capda()}} instead: canonical analysis of
principal coordinates (Anderson & Willis 2003) runs LDA on the first
few axes of \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}, choosing how many by how well they classify the
samples, and tests the separation of the groups by permutation.
\code{capda(dm, groups, n_pcoa_axes = 5)} gives the same scores as
\code{lda(pcoa(dm, n_axes = 5), groups)}. A few axes of \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}} also work.

\strong{Validate the separation}

LDA is supervised: it looks for the directions that separate the
groups, so its plots separate them, if not as readily as \code{\link[=plsda]{plsda()}}'s,
even when the groups don't differ. Don't read the plot as evidence of a
difference. Test it with a permutation test of the distances, such as
\code{\link[=permanova]{permanova()}}, or estimate the error rate of classifying new samples by
cross-validation.

\strong{Matching MASS and scikit-learn}

\code{lda()} gives the same scores, \code{scaling}, group means and proportion of
trace as \code{MASS::lda()} and its \code{predict()}, up to the sign of each axis,
and \code{eig} is the square of its \code{svd}. scikit-learn's
\code{LinearDiscriminantAnalysis(solver = "svd")} divides the within-group
covariance by \eqn{n} rather than \eqn{n - g}, so its \code{transform()}
scores and \code{scalings_} are larger by \eqn{\sqrt{n / (n - g)}}{sqrt(n / (n - g))};
its \code{explained_variance_ratio_} is \code{prop}. Its priors that don't add up
to 1 are rescaled with a warning; here they are an error.

\strong{Same names in other packages}

\code{MASS::lda()} shares its name with ecodive's \code{lda()}, and MASS is often
attached: whichever package was attached last masks the other's. Write
\code{ecodive::lda()}, or use \code{\link[=count_ord]{count_ord()}}, which always runs ecodive's, to
be sure. Elsewhere, "LDA" also means latent Dirichlet allocation (topic
models), and LEfSe's "LDA score" is an effect size from this method.

\strong{Axis signs and reproducibility}

An axis's sign is arbitrary, so different software can return mirror
images of the same axis. \code{lda()} flips each axis so that its largest
score in absolute value is positive, and flips its column of \code{scaling}
with it. Results are identical from run to run and for any number of
\code{cpus}.
}
\section{Ordination result}{

Every ordination function (\code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}, \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}}, \code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}}, \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}}, \code{\link[=capda]{capda()}},
\code{\link[=capscale]{capscale()}}, \code{\link[=mds]{mds()}}, \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}}, \code{\link[=isomap]{isomap()}}, \code{\link[=tsne]{tsne()}}, \code{\link[=umap]{umap()}}, and so
\code{\link[=dist_ord]{dist_ord()}}) returns the same kind of
object: a numeric matrix with one row per sample and one column per
axis, so that it can go straight into a data frame. Rows are
named after the samples, when the input has names. Columns are named
after the method: \code{"PCoA1"}, \code{"PC1"}, \code{"RPCA1"}, \code{"dbRDA1"} or \code{"CAP1"}
(then \code{"MDS1"} for unconstrained axes), \code{"MDS1"}, \code{"NMDS1"},
\code{"Isomap1"}, \code{"TSNE1"}, \code{"UMAP1"}, and so on.

Its attributes always begin with these three, in this order, each with
one value per column:
\itemize{
\item \code{method}: The method's ID, e.g. \code{"pcoa"} or \code{"nmds"}.
\item \code{eig}: Each axis's eigenvalue, on the method's own scale: \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}
and \code{\link[=isomap]{isomap()}} give the eigenvalues of Gower's centered matrix (sums
of squares); \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}} and \code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}} give variances, which are \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}'s
divided by \eqn{n - 1} for \eqn{n} samples; and \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}} and
\code{\link[=capscale]{capscale()}} follow \code{vegan}, which also divides by \eqn{n - 1} when any
distance is 4 or more. \code{NA} for \code{\link[=mds]{mds()}}, \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}}, \code{\link[=tsne]{tsne()}}, and
\code{\link[=umap]{umap()}}, which have no eigenvalues.
\item \code{prop}: The proportion of the total variance (or inertia) that each
axis explains. Unlike \code{eig}, it is comparable between methods, so use
it to label axes. \code{NA} where it isn't defined.
}

Attributes particular to the method follow them, and last \code{display},
with the ordination's title for \code{plot()} and \code{print()}: the method's
name, and for the methods that take distances, the distance matrix's
title, such as "Principal Coordinates Analysis of Bray-Curtis
Dissimilarity" (see \link{ecodive_display}). The matrix has class
\code{"ecodive_ord"} (as well as \code{"matrix"}), so that \code{print()} lists the
other attributes by name rather than in full, and \code{plot()} draws the
samples on the first two axes, labelled from the attributes, with any
loadings, constraints and centroids on top (see \code{\link[=plot.ecodive_ord]{plot.ecodive_ord()}}).
\code{plot(ord, "Species", iris)} colours the samples by a column of
metadata. Subsetting with \code{[}, or \code{as.data.frame()}, gives a plain
matrix or data frame without the attributes.
}

\section{Input Types}{


The \code{counts} parameter is designed to accept a simple numeric matrix, but
seamlessly supports objects from the following biological data packages:
\itemize{
\item \code{phyloseq}
\item \code{rbiom}
\item \code{SummarizedExperiment}
\item \code{TreeSummarizedExperiment}
}

For large datasets, standard matrix operations may be slow. See
\code{vignette('performance')} for details on using optimized formats
(e.g. sparse matrices) and parallel processing.
}

\section{Pseudocount}{

The \code{pseudocount} parameter is only relevant when \code{norm = 'clr'}.

Zeros are undefined in the centered log-ratio (CLR) transformation. If
\code{norm = 'clr'}, \code{pseudocount} is \code{NULL} (the default), and
zeros are detected, the function uses half the minimum non-zero value
(\code{min(x[x>0]) / 2}) and issues a warning.

To suppress the warning, provide an explicit value (e.g., \code{1}), or
replace the zeros first with \code{\link{replace_zeros}()}, whose
result needs no pseudocount.

\strong{Why this matters:} The choice of pseudocount is not neutral; it acts as
a weighting factor that can significantly distort downstream results,
especially for sparse datasets. See Gloor et al. (2017) and Kaul et al.
(2017) for open-access discussions on the mathematical implications, or
Costea et al. (2014) for the impact on community clustering.

See \code{\link{aitchison}} for references.
}

\examples{
    # LDA of iris: three groups, so two discriminant axes.
    ord <- lda(iris[, 1:4], 'Species', iris)
    plot(ord)
    attr(ord, 'prop')
    attr(ord, 'scaling')

    # Microbiome tables have more taxa than samples: use a few PCoA axes,
    # or capda(), which chooses how many.
    grp <- rep(c('a', 'b'), length.out = nrow(ex_counts))
    pc  <- pcoa(bray(ex_counts), n_axes = 2)
    lda(pc, grp)

    # Supervised: always validate the separation, e.g. with PERMANOVA.
    permanova(bray(ex_counts), grp, permutations = 99)

}
